<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Liang (555-587)</h1><p class="st-meta">555 CE – 587 CE · capital Jiangling</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small client state around Jiangling on the middle Yangtze, where a branch of the Liang imperial family reigned under Western Wei, Northern Zhou and Sui protection.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 554 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-wei">Western Wei</a> armies captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-liang">Emperor Yuan of Liang</a>. The following year they installed his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-cha">Xiao Cha</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a> and a rival of the Jiangling court, as emperor of a rump <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> state confined to the district around the city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its emperors kept the full ceremony of a Chinese court, with reign titles and officials, but a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-wei">Western Wei</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> garrison stood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a>, and the rulers depended entirely on their northern patrons. The territory was only a few prefectures wide. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-kui">Xiao Kui</a>, the second emperor, was a respected scholar, and his daughter married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Yang Guang</a>, the future Emperor Yang of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 587 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Emperor Wen of Sui</a> summoned the third ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-cong">Xiao Cong</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and abolished the state; some of its officials fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chen-dynasty">Chen dynasty</a>. The Xiao family remained prominent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> thanks to the marriage tie.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/western-liang-555-587.jpg" alt="Western Liang (555-587)" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_and_Southern_Dynasties_4.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>555 CE – 587 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiangling</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Xiao Cha (Emperor Xuan), Xiao Kui (Emperor Ming), Xiao Cong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Later Liang (555-587), Xiao Liang (Jiangling)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=571&amp;focus=western-liang-555-587" aria-label="Western Liang (555-587) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7600 1081 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Western Liang (555-587) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7920 1337L8057 1267M7920 1337L7769 1191M7920 1337L7769 1191M7920 1337L7769 1191"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8057 1267h0M7769 1191h0M7769 1191h0M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7920 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7920 1337h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=571&amp;focus=western-liang-555-587">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
